<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  WPML plugin configuration - read automatically by WPML when this plugin
  is active, to pre-fill Post Types Translation settings instead of
  requiring an admin to configure each one by hand.

  Locations and Bookings are not translatable (translate="0"): Locations
  are addresses/coordinates, not content that reads differently per
  language (GitHub issue #65); Bookings are transactional records, not
  content at all.

  Services and Guides are set to "Display as Translated" (translate="2"),
  NOT "Translatable" (translate="1") - this was the actual bug behind
  GitHub's WPML-structure report: "Translatable" makes WPML create a
  SEPARATE post (a separate ID) per language. That's fine for ordinary
  content, but wrong here, because:

  - A Guide post isn't just content - it's tied to a stateful
    availability calendar (wp_tc_guide_availability, keyed by that post's
    ID - see class-tc-availability.php) and to one specific WordPress
    login account (_tc_user_id). With three duplicate posts per guide
    (one per language), the calendar a guide manages through their own
    dashboard lives under ONE language's post ID, while a customer
    booking in a different language resolves a DIFFERENT post ID for
    "the same" guide - so blocked dates never stayed in sync between
    languages. This is exactly what was reported.
  - Guide<->Location/Service assignments (_tc_location_ids /
    _tc_service_ids) are post-ID references. Under "Translatable" they'd
    need to be re-resolved per language on every read/write (see
    TC_WPML::to_default_language_id() below) just to work around IDs that
    should never have differed in the first place.

  "Display as Translated" keeps a SINGLE canonical post - one ID, one
  calendar, one set of location/service assignments - while still
  letting a translator provide a per-language title and content (WPML's
  own Translation Editor handles this without creating a duplicate
  wp_posts row). That single-ID guarantee is also why
  TC_WPML::to_default_language_id() (class-tc-wpml.php, called from
  save_guide() in class-tc-meta-boxes.php and get_guides_for() in
  class-tc-availability.php) is now a no-op for every post type this
  plugin registers - Location and Booking were already untranslatable,
  and Service/Guide no longer duplicate either. Left in place as a
  harmless defensive layer rather than ripped out, in case that
  assumption ever needs revisiting.

  Known limitation: the extras repeater (_tc_extras on Service, a
  serialized array of label/price/max/description per row) has no
  per-language text of its own under this setup - it shows the same
  values in every language. WPML's field-translation tools are built for
  plain string values, not structured array data, so there's no clean
  way to route this through WPML as-is. If per-language extras text is
  needed later, that's a plugin feature to build (e.g. storing each
  extra's label/description as an array keyed by language code), not a
  WPML configuration change.

  Not yet verified against a real WPML install (this dev environment has
  no way to run one) - PROJECT_NOTES.md's "WPML support" section has the
  full history, including what to double-check first on a WPML-enabled
  staging site.
-->
<wpml-config>
	<custom-types>
		<custom-type translate="0">tc_location</custom-type>
		<custom-type translate="2">tc_service</custom-type>
		<custom-type translate="2">tc_guide</custom-type>
		<custom-type translate="0">tc_booking</custom-type>
	</custom-types>
</wpml-config>
